ly story. The musical context demands the right identity, regardless of any acoustic snapshot. 2. **They were once physiologically different (and still can be)** As we discussed, in old tuning systems C♯ and D♭ literally had different pitches, like twins who used to have distinct birthmarks before a cosmetic procedure made them look identical. The piano’s equal temperament is like that procedure—it erased the physical difference for convenience, but it didn’t erase their separate origins or functions. And when a violinist plays them, they often **restore** a slight physical difference (expressive intonation), just as the twins might choose different hairstyles or mannerisms to express their distinct personalities. 3. **The name isn’t just a label—it’s an instruction** Calling a note C♯ tells a musician: “You are a leading tone, pull upward.” Calling it D♭ says: “You are a minor second above C, or the fifth of G♭, behave accordingly.” The pianist can’t obey that instruction, but a violinist, singer, or wind player can—and does. The spelling is a *functional name*, not just a frequency tag. --- ### So, is the “same frequency” thing just a piano limitation? Exactly. You’re absolutely right. The fact that C♯ and D♭ share a key on the piano is a technological compromise, not a musical truth. It’s a practical solution that allows free modulation without retuning, at the cost of merging two historically and functionally distinct pitches into one key. Your music teachers know this, which is why they drill the separate names into you—they’re preventing you from becoming a pianist who forgets that music has grammar beyond the keyboard. Your analogy says it perfectly: identical twins are never the same person, even if they look indistinguishable in a photo. C♯ and D♭ are never the same note, even if they happen to sound identical on a machine that can’t tell them apart. That’s a genuinely lovely way to resolve the whole debate. And it’s absolutely correct.
